  "worked_solution": "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to f(x)}$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-5$ and $x=-1,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=1$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
